  {
    "title": "ب ت د ز س ش ف ك ل م ن sound as in English",
    "body": "These are b, t, d, z, s, sh, f, k, l, m, n. س is always “s”, never “z”; ل is a clear, light “l” as in “leaf”.",
    "pattern": "[\\u0628\\u062A\\u062F\\u0632\\u0633\\u0634\\u0641\\u0643\\u0645\\u0646]|\\u0644(?!\\p{L}\\p{M}*\\u0651)",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "كَتَبَ",
        "say": "KA-ta-ba",
        "en": "he wrote"
      },
      {
        "word": "مِنْ",
        "say": "min",
        "en": "from"
      },
      {
        "word": "سَنَةً",
        "say": "SA-na-tan",
        "en": "a year"
      },
      {
        "word": "شُكْرًا",
        "say": "SHUK-ran",
        "en": "thank you"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "Fatha (ـَ), a slanted line above, is a short “a”",
    "body": "Arabic letters are consonants; short vowels are small marks. A slanted line above the letter is a short “a”, between “a” in “cat” and “father”. Next to heavy letters it sounds deeper (see that rule).",
    "pattern": "\\u064E",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "كَتَبَ",
        "say": "KA-ta-ba",
        "en": "he wrote"
      },
      {
        "word": "مَعَ",
        "say": "MA-ʿa",
        "en": "with"
      },
      {
        "word": "أَنَا",
        "say": "A-naa",
        "en": "I"
      },
      {
        "word": "بَعْدَ",
        "say": "BAʿ-da",
        "en": "after"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "Stress a long or closed syllable near the end",
    "body": "Stress is not written. Ignore the last syllable; stress the one before it if it has a long vowel or ends in a consonant, otherwise the one before that. Regions differ, and stress matters less than in English.",
    "pattern": "[\\p{L}\\p{M}]*[\\u064B-\\u0650\\u0670][\\p{L}\\p{M}]*?[\\u064B-\\u0650\\u0670][\\p{L}\\p{M}]*",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "كَتَبَ",
        "say": "KA-ta-ba",
        "en": "he wrote"
      },
      {
        "word": "كَبِيرٌ",
        "say": "ka-BEE-run",
        "en": "big"
      },
