        "word": "يَقُولُ",
        "say": "ya-QOO-lu",
        "en": "he says"
      },
      {
        "word": "السُّوقِ",
        "say": "as-SOO-qi",
        "en": "the market"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "A long vowel shortens before a skipped ا",
    "body": "When a word ending in a long vowel (فِي, إِلَى, عَلَى) is followed by a skipped ا, the long vowel becomes short and runs straight into the next word: فِي الْبَيْتِ is “fil-BAY-ti”.",
    "pattern": "(?:\\u0650\\u064A|\\u064E[\\u0627\\u0649]|\\u064F\\u0648\\u0627?) (?=\\u0627\\p{L}(?:\\u0652|(?!\\p{M})))",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "فِي الْبَيْتِ",
        "say": "fil-BAY-ti",
        "en": "at home"
      },
      {
        "word": "فِي الصَّبَاحِ",
        "say": "fiṣ-ṣa-BAA-ḥi",
        "en": "in the morning"
      },
      {
        "word": "إِلَى الْبَيْتِ",
        "say": "i-lal-BAY-ti",
        "en": "(to) home"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "ص is a heavy “s”",
    "body": "ص is one of four heavy letters: say “s” with the back of the tongue raised, giving a deep, hollow sound. Compare س in “sat” with ص in “sod”. Written “ṣ” in the respellings.",
    "pattern": "\\u0635",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "صَبَاحًا",
        "say": "ṣa-BAA-ḥan",
        "en": "in the morning"
      },
      {
        "word": "صَغِيرٌ",
        "say": "ṣa-GHEE-run",
        "en": "small"
      },
      {
        "word": "الصَّيْفِ",
        "say": "aṣ-ṢAY-fi",
        "en": "the summer"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "ط is a heavy “t”",
    "body": "ط is the heavy partner of ت: a “t” with the back of the tongue raised, as in “top” rather than “tip”, and without a puff of air. Written “ṭ” in the respellings.",
    "pattern": "\\u0637",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "طَوِيلٌ",
        "say": "ṭa-WEE-lun",
        "en": "long, tall"
      },
      {
        "word": "طَبْعًا",
        "say": "ṬAB-ʿan",
        "en": "of course"
      },
      {
        "word": "فَقَطْ",
        "say": "FA-qaṭ",
        "en": "only"
      },
      {
        "word": "الطَّرِيقِ",
        "say": "aṭ-ṭa-REE-qi",
        "en": "the road"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "خ sounds like “ch” in Scottish “loch”",
    "body": "خ is a rough, scraping “kh” from the back of the mouth, like Scottish “loch” or German “Bach”.",
    "pattern": "\\u062E",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "خَمْسَ",
        "say": "KHAM-sa",
        "en": "five"
      },
      {
        "word": "الْخُبْزَ",
        "say": "al-KHUB-za",
        "en": "the bread"
      },
      {
        "word": "أُخْرَى",
        "say": "UKH-raa",
        "en": "other (feminine)"
      },
      {
        "word": "أَخِيرًا",
        "say": "a-KHEE-ran",
        "en": "finally"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "ى at the end of a word (alif maqsura) is a long “aa”",
    "body": "ى, a ي without dots, appears only at the end of a word and sounds like ا: a long “aa”. With a tanwin (ـًى) it is “-an”.",
    "pattern": "\\u0649",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "إِلَى",
        "say": "I-laa",
        "en": "to"
      },
      {
        "word": "عَلَى",
        "say": "ʿA-laa",
        "en": "on"
      },
      {
        "word": "لَيْلَى",
        "say": "LAY-laa",
        "en": "Layla (name)"
      },
      {
        "word": "مَقْهًى",
        "say": "MAQ-han",
        "en": "a café"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "ث sounds like “th” in “thin”",
    "body": "ث is the soft, breathy “th” of “thin” and “three”, never “t”.",
    "pattern": "\\u062B",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "ثُمَّ",
        "say": "THUM-ma",
        "en": "then"
      },
      {
        "word": "ثَلَاثَةِ",
        "say": "tha-LAA-tha-ti",
        "en": "three"
      },
      {
        "word": "الثَّانِي",
        "say": "ath-THAA-nee",
        "en": "the second"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "ذ sounds like “th” in “this”",
    "body": "ذ is the buzzing “th” of “this” and “mother”, never “d” or “z”. Written “dh” in the respellings.",
    "pattern": "\\u0630",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "هٰذَا",
        "say": "HAA-dhaa",
        "en": "this"
      },
      {
        "word": "ذَهَبَ",
        "say": "DHA-ha-ba",
        "en": "he went"
      },
      {
        "word": "إِذَا",
        "say": "I-dhaa",
        "en": "if"
      },
      {
        "word": "مُنْذُ",
        "say": "MUN-dhu",
        "en": "since, ago"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "ض is a heavy “d”",
    "body": "ض is the heavy partner of د: a “d” with the back of the tongue raised, as in “dodge” rather than “dad”. Written “ḍ” in the respellings.",
    "pattern": "\\u0636",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "أَيْضًا",
        "say": "AY-ḍan",
        "en": "also"
      },
      {
        "word": "ضَحِكَ",
        "say": "ḌA-ḥi-ka",
        "en": "he laughed"
      },
      {
        "word": "الْأَرْضِ",
        "say": "al-'AR-ḍi",
        "en": "the ground, the earth"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "غ is a gargled “gh”, like a French r",
    "body": "غ is the buzzing, voiced partner of خ, made in the same place: close to the “r” of Parisian French. Written “gh” in the respellings.",
    "pattern": "\\u063A",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "صَغِيرٌ",
        "say": "ṣa-GHEE-run",
        "en": "small"
      },
      {
        "word": "غَدًا",
        "say": "GHA-dan",
        "en": "tomorrow"
      },
      {
        "word": "الْفَرَاغِ",
        "say": "al-fa-RAA-ghi",
        "en": "free time, emptiness"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "A small upright alif (ـٰ) is a hidden long “aa”",
    "body": "A few very common words write their long “aa” as a tiny vertical stroke above the letter instead of a full alif. Read it exactly like ا.",
    "pattern": "\\u0670",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "هٰذَا",
        "say": "HAA-dhaa",
        "en": "this"
      },
      {
        "word": "هٰذِهِ",
        "say": "HAA-dhi-hi",
        "en": "this (feminine)"
      },
      {
        "word": "ذٰلِكَ",
        "say": "DHAA-li-ka",
        "en": "that"
      },
      {
        "word": "لٰكِنَّ",
        "say": "laa-KIN-na",
        "en": "but"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "ظ is a heavy “th” as in “this”",
    "body": "ظ is the heavy partner of ذ: the “th” of “this” with the back of the tongue raised. Many speakers say it as a heavy “z”. Written “ẓ” in the respellings.",
    "pattern": "\\u0638",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "الظُّهْرِ",
        "say": "aẓ-ẒUH-ri",
        "en": "noon"
      },
      {
        "word": "نَظَرَ",
        "say": "NA-ẓa-ra",
        "en": "he looked"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "آ (alif with madda) is hamza plus a long “aa”",
    "body": "The wavy line on آ stands for a hamza followed by a long “aa”: a catch in the throat, then “aa”.",
    "pattern": "\\u0622",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "الْآنَ",
        "say": "al-'AA-na",
        "en": "now"
      },
      {
        "word": "آخِرِ",
        "say": "AA-khi-ri",
        "en": "the end of, last"
      },
      {
        "word": "آسِفٌ",
        "say": "AA-si-fun",
        "en": "sorry"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "ا after a final و (ـُوا) is silent",
    "body": "Verbs ending in “-oo” for “they” are written with an extra alif after the و. It is only a spelling mark; do not read it.",
    "pattern": "(?<=[\\u064E\\u064F]\\u0651?)\\u0648\\u0652?\\u0627(?![\\p{L}\\p{M}])",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "كَانُوا",
        "say": "KAA-noo",
        "en": "they were"
      },
      {
        "word": "قَالُوا",
        "say": "QAA-loo",
        "en": "they said"
      },
      {
        "word": "وَصَلُوا",
        "say": "WA-ṣa-loo",
        "en": "they arrived"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "اللّٰه is said with a deep, heavy “l”",
    "body": "In the word for God (Allah) the double l is deep and dark, as in “full”, unless an “i” comes just before it, as in لِلّٰهِ.",
    "pattern": "\\u0644\\u0651\\u0670\\u0647",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "إِنْ شَاءَ اللّٰهُ",
        "say": "in SHAA-'al-LAA-hu",
        "en": "God willing"
      },
      {
        "word": "عَبْدُ اللّٰهِ",
        "say": "ʿAB-dul-LAA-hi",
        "en": "Abdullah (name)"
      },
      {
        "word": "الْحَمْدُ لِلّٰهِ",
        "say": "al-ḤAM-du lil-LAA-hi",
        "en": "thank God (light l)"
      }
    ]
  }
]
